.TH STYXBENCH 1
.SH NAME
styxbench \- time a Styx round trip against a raw TCP exchange
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxbench
[
.BI -n " count"
]
[
.BI -s " size"
]
[
.BI -r " rpcaddr"
]
.I rawaddr file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Styxbench
times
.I count
exchanges of
.I size
bytes several ways against the same server, and prints microseconds each
and the ratios: a write-and-read on a bare TCP connection to
.IR rawaddr ;
sequential reads of
.IR file ,
which is on a mounted namespace; and reads of
.I file
at offsets a megabyte apart, so that the mount driver's readahead
cannot answer one from another.
.PP
It exists for the section of
.B doc/inferno-cloud-modern.ms
that proposes to measure the claim the rest of the plan rests on: that
distribution in the operating system is cheaper than distribution
assembled above one.  A namespace mounted across a network is the form
that claim takes, and a Styx round trip is its price.
.PP
Both halves must be answered by the same kind of server or the ratio
measures the server and not the protocol.  Use
.IR zerofs (1)
on the Styx side against
.IR tcpserve (1)
.B -e
on the raw side: both are then Limbo, and both pay Dis interpretation
per exchange..PP
With
.BR -r ,
a further arm: a conventional RPC against
.IR rpcserve (1)
at
.IR rpcaddr .
Each iteration formats a length-prefixed request, sends it, reads the
reply's length, parses its header line and checks that the announced
payload length agrees.  That is the arm
.B doc/inferno-cloud-modern.ms
item 8 asks for \(em raw TCP is the floor, with no protocol at all, and
is not what anyone would deploy.
.PP
It runs over one connection, reused, exactly as the Styx and raw arms
do.  Using
.IR httpclient (2)
instead would have measured a new TCP connection for every request,
because it sends
.BR "Connection: close" ,
and charged Styx nothing for the connection it already holds.
.PP
The
.B jumping
arm is the one to compare against
.BR rpc :
it strides far enough that the mount driver's readahead cannot answer
one read from another, so each iteration is a real
.B Tread
on the wire, which is what an RPC call is.  The sequential arm is
cheaper than either because readahead answers most of it, and that is a
property of the file abstraction rather than of the protocol.

.SH BUGS
The transport matters and is not controlled.  Measured through QEMU's
user-mode networking the absolute figures swing by a factor of two
between runs \(em raw exchanges from 1056 to 2040 microseconds \(em
while the ratio holds.  Quote the ratio, not the microseconds, and say
what the transport was.
.PP
At 300 iterations the ratio itself is noise: it ranged from 0.3 to 1.3
across runs and only settled at 3000.  An iteration count here has to
be justified rather than assumed.
.PP
The client is a hosted
.IR emu (1)
and the server a native kernel.  That asymmetry cancels, because both
halves use the same client, but it means these are not two like
machines and the plan's own version of the experiment \(em two
instances on a real network \(em has not been run.
